105 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 76 0 R
/E (Machine Learning \(ML\) is a computational approach in which a program does not follow hand- written rules for every case, but instead infers the rules from examples \(data\). The program is given experience in the form of data, and an algorithm search for a general pattern that explains that data. The learned pattern is then applied to new, unseen cases. According to Tom Mitchell, a program learns from experience E with respect to a task T and performance measure P if its performance at T, measured by P, improves with E. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
106 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 76 0 R
/E (Formally, in supervised learning we are given a training set of examples {\(x1, y1\), ..., \(xn, yn\)}, where x is the input \(features\) and y are the output. The learner searches a set of candidate functions called the hypothesis space H and selects a hypothesis h that maps x to y and minimises a loss \(error\) on the training data, in the hope that it will also perform well on new data. This ability to perform well on unseen data is called generalisation, and it is the real goal of learning. )
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [15 20 16 21 17 22 18 23 19]
>>
endobj
