/T (Q10. Discuss the ethical, social, and technical issues that may arise when deploying Machine Learning systems. Include algorithmic bias, privacy, explainability, security, fairness, accountability, and model drift in your discussion. )
/E (Q10. Discuss the ethical, social, and technical issues that may arise when deploying Machine Learning systems. Include algorithmic bias, privacy, explainability, security, fairness, accountability, and model drift in your discussion. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [33]
>>
endobj
638 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (When ML systems are used in real life for decisions about people \(jobs, loans, health, policing, education\), their errors and side effects affect individuals and society. The main issues are as follows. )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [34]
>>
endobj
639 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 88 0 R
/T (1. Algorithmic Bias )
/E (1. Algorithmic Bias )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [35]
>>
endobj
640 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 88 0 R
/E (Bias occurs when a model produces systematically unfair or skewed results because of biased training data, unrepresentative samples, biased labels or design choices. Example: an experimental recruiting tool developed by a large company was reported to have been dropped after it was found to favour male candidates, because it had learned from past hiring data dominated by men; face )
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [36]
>>
endobj
641 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (recognition systems have been reported to have higher error rates for darker-skinned women. Mitigation: representative data, bias audits, re-weighting, diverse teams and testing on subgroups. 2. Privacy )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
642 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (ML often needs large amounts of personal and sensitive data \(health, location, financial and behavioural data\). Risks include unauthorised collection, misuse, data breaches, re-identification of anonymised data and leakage of training data from models \(membership inference, model inversion\). Mitigation: consent, data minimisation, anonymisation, encryption, access control, differential privacy, federated learning and compliance with laws such as GDPR and India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
643 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 89 0 R
/T (3. Explainability \(Transparency\) )
/E (3. Explainability \(Transparency\) )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
644 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (Many powerful models \(deep networks, ensembles\) act as "black boxes". If a loan is rejected or a disease is diagnosed, people and regulators need to know why. Lack of explanation reduces trust, makes debugging and detecting bias difficult, and may violate the right to explanation. Mitigation: use interpretable models where possible, and tools such as feature importance, SHAP and LIME, documentation \(model cards\) and clear communication. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
645 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 89 0 R
/T (4. Security )
/E (4. Security )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
646 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (ML systems face special attacks: adversarial examples \(small changes in input that fool the model\), data poisoning \(corrupting training data\), model theft and evasion by fraudsters or malware authors. Mitigation: adversarial training, input validation, secure pipelines, access control, monitoring and regular security testing. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
647 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 89 0 R
/T (5. Fairness )
/E (5. Fairness )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
648 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (Fairness means that the system treats individuals and groups equitably and does not discriminate on the basis of gender, caste, religion, race, age or income. It is difficult because there are several formal definitions \(demographic parity, equal opportunity, equalised odds\) that cannot all be satisfied together, and removing a sensitive attribute is not enough as other features may act as proxies. Mitigation: define fairness goals with stakeholders, measure group-wise performance, and apply fairness-aware training or post-processing. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
649 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 89 0 R
/T (6. Accountability )
/E (6. Accountability )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
650 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (When an automated decision causes harm, it must be clear who is responsible: the developer, the data provider, the deploying organisation or the user. Lack of accountability leads to harm without remedy. Mitigation: clear ownership and governance, documentation and audit trails, human-in- the-loop for high-stakes decisions, the right to appeal, impact assessments and compliance with regulation \(such as the EU AI Act\). )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
651 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 89 0 R
/T (7. Model Drift )
/E (7. Model Drift )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
652 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 89 0 R
/E (After deployment, the real-world data changes. Data drift is a change in the distribution of inputs, and concept drift is a change in the relationship between inputs and outputs. Accuracy therefore degrades silently. Example: a demand forecasting or credit model trained before a pandemic fails when behaviour changes; fraud patterns change as fraudsters adapt. Mitigation: continuous monitoring, drift detection, periodic retraining and validation, and fallback rules. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
